1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,803
This is the same account you've been following since Section 0, read straight

2
00:00:04,803 --> 00:00:07,868
through as one pass instead of six separate lectures.

3
00:00:07,868 --> 00:00:12,378
Watch for how each stage's output becomes the next stage's input, because that

4
00:00:12,378 --> 00:00:15,790
handoff is exactly what your own capstone has to reproduce.

5
00:00:15,790 --> 00:00:20,532
It starts with the product truth doc from Section 2.1: fifty-eight dollars for the

6
00:00:20,532 --> 00:00:25,390
three-pack, thirty-four dollars of contribution margin, and the claims boundary Dana

7
00:00:25,390 --> 00:00:29,785
holds hard, the fifteen to twenty mmHg spec, nothing about medical outcomes.

8
00:00:29,785 --> 00:00:32,908
Every later decision traces back to this one document.

9
00:00:32,908 --> 00:00:37,708
Sam wasn't in the room when Dana first wrote that boundary down, but he didn't need

10
00:00:37,708 --> 00:00:38,055
to be.

11
00:00:38,055 --> 00:00:42,912
It's specific enough, and written down clearly enough, that every brief, every hook,

12
00:00:42,912 --> 00:00:47,654
every batch built afterward could simply check against it rather than needing Dana

13
00:00:47,654 --> 00:00:50,199
to personally approve every individual line.

14
00:00:50,199 --> 00:00:54,709
That's what a real product truth doc actually buys a team: a shared, checkable

15
00:00:54,709 --> 00:00:57,890
reference, not a meeting that has to happen every time.

16
00:00:57,890 --> 00:01:02,574
One SKU, one document, and everything from here forward, twenty angles, dozens of

17
00:01:02,574 --> 00:01:07,316
statics, several video cuts, all of it traces back to these same handful of facts.

18
00:01:07,316 --> 00:01:11,827
Nothing downstream introduces a new claim the product truth doc didn't already

19
00:01:11,827 --> 00:01:12,289
license.

20
00:01:12,839 --> 00:01:17,691
Twenty angles, not one hero ad, because Section 1's volume maths already told you a

21
00:01:17,691 --> 00:01:20,321
single guess wasn't going to find the winner.

22
00:01:20,321 --> 00:01:24,939
Each angle here traces back to a real fact from the product truth doc, never an

23
00:01:24,939 --> 00:01:27,044
invented claim layered on top of it.

24
00:01:27,044 --> 00:01:30,434
Notice the awareness ladder doing real work in this table.

25
00:01:30,434 --> 00:01:35,110
The same compression spec gets framed completely differently for a cold audience

26
00:01:35,110 --> 00:01:39,962
who's never heard of graduated compression than for someone who's already searching

27
00:01:39,962 --> 00:01:44,522
"socks for a twelve-hour shift." Same product, same claims boundary, different

28
00:01:44,522 --> 00:01:45,048
argument.

29
00:01:45,048 --> 00:01:49,432
Notice, too, how little of this table required anything genuinely new to be

30
00:01:49,432 --> 00:01:49,958
invented.

31
00:01:49,958 --> 00:01:54,810
The hook taxonomy already existed as a general framework before Harlan's brief ever

32
00:01:54,810 --> 00:01:55,511
got written.

33
00:01:55,511 --> 00:01:59,953
The angle map is what happens when that general framework meets one specific

34
00:01:59,953 --> 00:02:01,181
product's real facts.

35
00:02:01,181 --> 00:02:06,091
Twenty angles isn't twenty separate creative ideas dreamed up individually, it's one

36
00:02:06,091 --> 00:02:10,826
framework applied systematically until it runs out of honest combinations to try.

37
00:02:10,826 --> 00:02:14,801
That systematic quality is exactly why this scales past one product.

38
00:02:14,801 --> 00:02:19,594
The same three rows, hook taxonomy, awareness ladder, angle map, apply to whatever

39
00:02:19,594 --> 00:02:24,154
you're building your own capstone on, producing a different twenty angles from

40
00:02:24,154 --> 00:02:26,784
different facts, using the identical process.

41
00:02:27,334 --> 00:02:29,698
Notice all three failures share something.

42
00:02:29,698 --> 00:02:31,556
None of them were caught by luck.

43
00:02:31,556 --> 00:02:36,228
Brand control checking, text-on-image review, and licence verification are specific

44
00:02:36,228 --> 00:02:40,900
disciplines Sections 3 and 4 taught for exactly this reason, and all three caught a

45
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:43,602
real problem before it ever reached an audience.

46
00:02:43,602 --> 00:02:48,330
This is also the moment worth sitting with if your own capstone hasn't hit a failure

47
00:02:48,330 --> 00:02:49,118
like this yet.

48
00:02:49,118 --> 00:02:51,257
It doesn't mean your process is worse.

49
00:02:51,257 --> 00:02:55,760
It might mean you haven't generated enough variants yet to find where the tool's

50
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:56,886
limits actually are.

51
00:02:56,886 --> 00:03:01,164
These failures are common, not a sign Harlan's batch was unusually troubled.

52
00:03:01,164 --> 00:03:04,035
Look again at how each failure was actually caught.

53
00:03:04,035 --> 00:03:08,707
Brand colour got checked against the product truth doc's real values, not eyeballed

54
00:03:08,707 --> 00:03:13,154
for "close enough." The spec overlay got read at full size, not glanced at in a

55
00:03:13,154 --> 00:03:13,998
thumbnail grid.

56
00:03:13,998 --> 00:03:18,388
The avatar's licence terms got checked line by line before anything was recast

57
00:03:18,388 --> 00:03:18,951
around it.

58
00:03:18,951 --> 00:03:21,484
None of these catches happened automatically.

59
00:03:21,484 --> 00:03:25,819
Each one needed someone to actually apply the specific check Sections 3 and 4

60
00:03:25,819 --> 00:03:29,140
taught, deliberately, on every single variant in the batch.

61
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:33,587
That's worth remembering the next time a batch of twenty looks mostly fine at a

62
00:03:33,587 --> 00:03:33,981
glance.

63
00:03:33,981 --> 00:03:37,358
Mostly fine is exactly the condition these checks exist for.

64
00:03:37,358 --> 00:03:41,129
A batch that's plainly broken doesn't need a checklist to catch it.

65
00:03:41,679 --> 00:03:44,007
This is the batch's most important catch.

66
00:03:44,007 --> 00:03:48,321
A forty percent swelling claim nobody ever tested, invented somewhere in the

67
00:03:48,321 --> 00:03:53,089
drafting process, would have been the single fastest way to a policy strike or worse

68
00:03:53,089 --> 00:03:54,111
if it had shipped.

69
00:03:54,111 --> 00:03:58,198
Section 6.6's checklist caught it before a dollar of spend went near it.

70
00:03:58,198 --> 00:04:02,512
Notice what replaced it: not a softer version of the same invented number, a

71
00:04:02,512 --> 00:04:05,747
completely different claim, one Dana can actually defend.

72
00:04:05,747 --> 00:04:08,188
The compression spec was always defensible.

73
00:04:08,188 --> 00:04:09,778
The forty percent never was.

74
00:04:09,778 --> 00:04:13,524
That's the difference pre-flight exists to catch, not just soften.

75
00:04:13,524 --> 00:04:18,122
Notice, too, that this catch happened at the human review gate Section 6.5 built,

76
00:04:18,122 --> 00:04:19,144
not automatically.

77
00:04:19,144 --> 00:04:23,742
A checklist only works if someone actually runs it, on every draft, including the

78
00:04:23,742 --> 00:04:25,331
ones that read convincingly.

79
00:04:25,331 --> 00:04:28,794
The forty percent claim was written fluently and confidently.

80
00:04:28,794 --> 00:04:32,654
It was still false, and confidence was never the test that mattered.

81
00:04:32,654 --> 00:04:34,129
The product truth doc was.

82
00:04:34,129 --> 00:04:38,898
The disclosure caption on the avatar video is a smaller catch by comparison, but the

83
00:04:38,898 --> 00:04:40,487
same discipline produced it.

84
00:04:40,487 --> 00:04:45,028
A synthetic performer appearing in a paid ad, without disclosure, was a real gap

85
00:04:45,028 --> 00:04:49,626
Section 6.3 exists to close, checked and fixed the same way the claim was, before

86
00:04:49,626 --> 00:04:51,897
either one ever reached a real audience.

87
00:04:52,447 --> 00:04:54,701
One honest note before moving into testing.

88
00:04:54,701 --> 00:04:58,423
Nothing about this walkthrough compresses cleanly into a real timeline.

89
00:04:58,423 --> 00:05:02,565
Dana and Sam's actual product truth doc took several sittings to get right, the

90
00:05:02,565 --> 00:05:06,707
batch went through more than one generation pass before twenty statics actually

91
00:05:06,707 --> 00:05:11,006
cleared review, and pre-flight caught the swelling claim only because someone read

92
00:05:11,006 --> 00:05:14,834
every draft carefully rather than skimming the ones that looked finished.

93
00:05:14,834 --> 00:05:18,818
None of that shows up when the whole sequence gets narrated back in minutes.

94
00:05:18,818 --> 00:05:21,072
Hold that against your own capstone's pace.

95
00:05:21,072 --> 00:05:25,162
If your version of this phase is taking longer than watching this lecture did,

96
00:05:25,162 --> 00:05:26,840
that's not a sign you're behind.

97
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:31,244
It's a sign you're doing it at the speed real work actually happens at, which is the

98
00:05:31,244 --> 00:05:34,809
only speed that produces a batch worth testing honestly in part two.

99
00:05:35,359 --> 00:05:39,796
Next lecture picks up exactly where this one ends: this cleared batch, built into a

100
00:05:39,796 --> 00:05:43,966
real testing architecture, and read honestly once the numbers actually arrive.

101
00:05:43,966 --> 00:05:48,243
If you're partway through your own capstone brief, pause here and check your own

102
00:05:48,243 --> 00:05:52,360
product truth doc and batch against everything this pass just walked through.

103
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:56,744
The order matters more than any individual step, and seeing it run start to finish

104
00:05:56,744 --> 00:06:00,059
is the whole point of watching this before finishing your own.

105
00:06:00,059 --> 00:06:02,464
Nothing in this pass was dramatic on its own.

106
00:06:02,464 --> 00:06:06,047
A document, a table, two caught rendering errors, one caught claim.

107
00:06:06,047 --> 00:06:10,431
Put together, in order, that's the entire reason Harlan's batch was ready to spend

108
00:06:10,431 --> 00:06:14,654
real money on by the time Section 7's testing architecture got built around it.

109
00:06:14,654 --> 00:06:19,145
That quiet, checkable thoroughness is the actual skill this course has been teaching

110
00:06:19,145 --> 00:06:20,000
since Section 0.

111
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,384
Which of these five stages would you be most tempted to rush on your own capstone?

112
00:06:24,384 --> 00:06:28,822
For most people it's pre-flight, because by that point the batch already exists and

113
00:06:28,822 --> 00:06:32,832
looks finished, and checking it carefully feels like it's only slowing down

114
00:06:32,832 --> 00:06:34,382
something that's ready to go.

115
00:06:34,382 --> 00:06:38,873
That's exactly the moment Harlan's forty percent claim would have shipped, if nobody

116
00:06:38,873 --> 00:06:40,691
had resisted that same temptation.
